    "readout_text": "To determine the probability that Flora the frog eventually lands on position 10, we model the situation using a recursive probability function.\n\n---\n\n### Problem Setup\n\nFlora starts at position 0 on the number line and makes jumps to the right. Each jump distance $ m $ is a positive integer with probability $ \\frac{1}{2^m} $. We define:\n\n- $ P(n) $: the probability that Flora eventually reaches position 10 starting from position $ n $.\n\n---\n\n### Base Cases\n\n- If $ n = 10 $, then Flora is already at the target, so $ P(10) = 1 $.\n- If $ n > 10 $, Flora can only jump to the right, so she can never return to 10. Thus, $ P(n) = 0 $ for all $ n > 10 $.\n\n---\n\n### Recurrence Relation\n\nFor $ n < 10 $, the probability $ P(n) $ is given by:\n\n$$\nP(n) = \\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^m} \\cdot P(n + m)\n$$\n\nThis means that from position $ n $, Flora can jump any distance $ m $, and from there, she has probability $ P(n + m) $ of eventually reaching 10.\n\n---\n\n### Key Insight\n\nBy computing $ P(n) $ for $ n = 9, 8, 7, \\dots, 0 $, we observe a **pattern**: for all $ n < 10 $, the value of $ P(n) $ consistently evaluates to $ \\frac{1}{2} $. This pattern holds because the structure of the recurrence and the probabilities are symmetric and uniform across the range $ n < 10 $, leading to a consistent probability of $ \\frac{1}{2} $ for all such $ n $.\n\n---\n\n### Final Calculation\n\nSince Flora starts at position 0, we are interested in $ P(0) $. As shown above, for all $ n < 10 $, $ P(n) = \\frac{1}{2} $, so:\n\n$$\nP(0) = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\nThe probability is $ \\frac{1}{2} $, which is already in simplest form. So, $ m = 1 $, $ n = 2 $, and $ m + n = 3 $.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{3}\n$$\n\n\\confidence{Almost certain}",
